= Semantic similarity — Technical =

== Metric choice ==
A similarity function has the form:

<pre>
sim : X × X → ℝ
</pre>

but need not be a mathematical metric. Many similarity functions are not distances and may violate metric axioms.

A derived distance such as 1 − cosine similarity still requires care because geometry and normalization affect interpretation.

== Taxonomic measures ==
Ontology/lexical-resource measures can use:

* path length;
* least common subsumer depth;
* information content;
* feature overlap.

These depend on the structure and coverage of the chosen taxonomy.

== Distributional measures ==
Embedding methods commonly use cosine similarity, dot product, learned bilinear functions, or neural scoring.

Given normalized vectors:

<pre>
cos(x,y) = x · y
</pre>

High geometric similarity reflects the representation and training objective, not a theory-independent semantic fact.

== Text similarity ==
Semantic textual similarity can be modeled with cross-encoders, bi-encoders, sentence embeddings, alignment models, or task-specific regressors/classifiers.

Lexical overlap is insufficient for phenomena such as negation, scope, contradiction, reference, and paraphrase.

== Annotation ==
Human-rated datasets require instructions that distinguish similarity from relatedness. Inter-annotator disagreement is substantive evidence about concept/task ambiguity rather than mere noise.

